  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe mid-week synoptic pattern remains on track from previous\nforecasts, with anomalous ridging over the SE CONUS, and upper \nlows near the Hudson Bay as well as the Pacific NW. This pattern \nwill keep the monsoonal moisture plume focused across northwest \nportions of the FA, and precipitable water (PWAT) values nearing \n1.5\" will be well above climatology (above the 95th percentile). A\nsouthward moving cold front associated with the Hudson Bay sfc \nlow and a mid- level disturbance emanating from New Mexico will \nsupport likely PoPs (60-80%) across NW portions of the FA Tues PM,\nwith chance PoPs (40- 50%) across much of the Caprock. Forecast \nsoundings depict less downdraft CAPE than previous days, which \ncould lower the strong wind gusts potential, although water-loaded\ndowndrafts may still be capable of sporadic strong winds.\n\nBetween Wed-Fri, the upper ridge gradually shifts back west,\nreaching eastern OK/TX by Fri. That will result in subtle height \nrises and shunt the monsoonal moisture axis a tad west, so while \ndaily storm chances remain, they are largely confined to areas \nnear the TX/NM border and the far SW TX Panhandle. Temps remain \nabove normal through the work week, although a gradual cooling \ntrend is anticipated with highs in the upper 80s/low 90s on the \nCaprock by Fri, and the mid 90s off the Caprock.\n\nFor late week and the weekend, deterministic guidance has trended\nseveral degrees warmer compared to previous runs. This trend is \nnot unique to the deterministic models either, with the model \nblend exhibiting this same trend. This is due to a slower \nevolution of yet another closed low over the Pacific NW. Previous \nmodel runs had this wave emerging onto the central Plains by Fri, \npushing a cold front southward across W TX. This system is no \nlonger progged to reach the Plains by Fri, and its exact timing \nover the weekend appears less certain. The cooling trend is still \nforecast to continue into the weekend, with seasonable values \nfavored by Sun (mid 80s), but the potential for a more dramatic \ncool down with below normal temps now looks less likely. While the\nprimary upper low is slower, occasional weak disturbances \nemanating from NM within the SW flow regime will keep conditions \nunsettled, with occasional precip chances Fri through the weekend.\nThe blend has near uniform PoPs area-wide, but based on the \nsynoptic setup I suspect we'll eventually see a gradient in the \nPoPs, with higher chances on the Caprock.",
